  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed pathway by which inflammation and hyperammonaemia interact to damage brain function. At the top, these two conditions are shown as linked drivers of cellular stress in the brain. One branch highlights activation of microglia and astrocytes, with increased inducible nitric oxide synthase, pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-1β and IL-6, and prostaglandin E2. This glial reactivity is associated with altered surface expression of neurotransmitter receptors, including AMPA, NMDA, and GABA receptors, implying disrupted neurotransmission.\n\nA second branch shows hyperammonaemia promoting nitric oxide production through NMDA receptor activation and increasing reactive oxygen species via NADPH oxidase. These changes converge on oxidative and nitrosative stress. The diagram notes that this stress modifies proteins and RNA, changes gene expression and signalling, and can induce senescence.\n\nBoth the inflammatory/glial pathway and the oxidative stress pathway feed into a final outcome illustrated by a highlighted brain region: impaired cognitive and motor function. Overall, the image presents a mechanistic model in which ammonia excess and inflammation reinforce each other, leading to neuroinflammation, oxidative damage, altered synaptic signalling, and ultimately neurological dysfunction.",
